WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.809 --> 00:00:16.700
NETFLIX CASH SALARIES:
Did you know that Netflix doesn’t offer

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:19.240
traditional health insurance to their employees?

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:23.380
Instead, Netflix offers a $15,000 yearly allowance.

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:28.430
If you want, you can use this with one of
Netflix’s Kaiser or Collective Health plans,

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:32.149
but you can also use the allowance with any
provider you want.

00:00:32.149 --> 00:00:37.450
And if you end up spending less than $15,000
on health insurance, you can even get up to

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:38.469
$5,000 back.

00:00:38.469 --> 00:00:43.199
And it’s not just you’re health insurance
benefit that you can get in cash either, you

00:00:43.199 --> 00:00:48.609
can actually get all of your benefits in the
form of cash even including your stock compensation.

00:00:48.609 --> 00:00:54.329
This approach has resulted in Netflix paying
the highest cash salaries in the world period.

00:00:54.329 --> 00:01:00.430
Basically imagine Google tier compensation
but all in the form of cash, that’s Netflix.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:05.190
Combine this with the fact that Netflix tries
to provide each employee with 100th percentile

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:08.550
salaries, and this gets crazy real fast.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:10.540
Take software engineers for example.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:14.350
The highest paid software engineer at Netflix
who has submitted their salary to levels is

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:19.619
a back end engineer located in Chicago earning
$1.5 million per year.

00:01:19.619 --> 00:01:24.560
If you take a closer look, you’ll see that
the salary consists of $1.5 million in cash,

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:27.830
0 in stock, and 0 in bonus.

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:30.960
Google has similarly paid engineers as well.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:36.620
The highest paid engineer at Google comes
in at $1.27 million per year, but if you take

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:43.479
a closer look, you’ll see that “only”
$340,000 of that is in cash. $794,000 is in

00:01:43.479 --> 00:01:47.250
the form of stock, and $139,000 is in the
form of a bonus.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:50.450
And this is not just the case for software
engineers either.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:56.759
Whether you’re a technical program manager,
a product manager, or even the CEO, you’re

00:01:56.759 --> 00:02:00.140
compensation can be taken entirely in the
form of cash.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:05.869
Last year, Netflix’ co CEO Ted Sarandos,
for example, received $20 million in cash.

00:02:05.869 --> 00:02:09.539
This makes Netflix the highest cash payer
in the world period.

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:14.860
There may be some exceptions here and there
that make more than what Netflix offers, but

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:19.850
as an organization, no one pays higher cash
salaries than Netflix.

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:22.710
This doesn’t mean that you’re forced to
take cash though.

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:27.560
If you want to take stock, you can let HR
know, and they can convert your entire compensation

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:28.600
to stock.

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:33.190
The founder of Netflix, Reed Hastings, for
example, chose to take his $40 million package

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:34.210
mostly in stock.

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:37.630
Most employees, however, generally choose
fully cash compensations.

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:43.670
So, here’s how Netflix ended up becoming
the cash king and the pros and cons of this

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:45.150
practice.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:52.220
NETFLIX PHILOSOPHY:
These crazy high numbers are no doubt unorthodox,

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:56.750
but that’s just one of the many practices
at Netflix that are unorthodox.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:03:01.500
Reed Hastings has an extremely unique leadership
style that’s centered around freedom, responsibility,

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.060
and transparency.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:07.120
He likes to be brutally honest and isn’t
afraid to say things how they are.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:12.390
For example, you often hear CEOs refer to
their employees as being a family, but Reed

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:14.230
knows that that’s not true.

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:17.750
There’s only one reason you’re there:
for money.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:22.520
Hopefully, you somewhat like the work and
what the company is trying to accomplish,

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:24.550
but those are just pluses.

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:28.870
The bottom line is that you’re there for
money and the company has you there so that

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:30.310
they can make more money.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:31.590
Thats it.

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:37.890
Reed is super candid about this and is straightforward
in saying that Netflix is a team, not a family.

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:41.620
And all of his company’s policies are based
around this reality.

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:46.730
Not forcing stock compensation or health insurance
onto employees is part of this approach.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:52.610
As CEO, it’s natural to think that you’re
stock is the best, but Reed understands that

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:56.439
not all of his employees would agree, so he
doesn’t force it.

00:03:56.439 --> 00:04:00.439
Similarly, he doesn’t pay employees based
on how hard they work or how much time they

00:04:00.439 --> 00:04:01.950
spend in the office.

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:06.730
In fact, Netflix has no 9 to 5 policy or vacation
policy.

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:11.170
Instead, employees are given a task, expectations,
and a deadline.

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:15.920
If you’re able to perform to these standards
while working 10 hours a week, good for you.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:20.260
On the other hand, even if you’re working
100 hours per week, but you don’t even get

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:25.190
close to what’s expected, they’re gonna
look for someone else, it’s that simple.

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:29.280
Given this philosophy, it makes sense why
Netflix gives their employees so much control

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:31.260
over their compensations.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:35.139
But, having this philosophy isn’t enough
to utilize this strategy.

00:04:35.139 --> 00:04:39.620
The second thing to note is that Netflix is
actually in a position where they can afford

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:40.620
it.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.690
Just because you’re a big company doesn’t
mean that you can afford it.

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:48.630
Amazon, for example, has extraordinarily low
profit margins, so Amazon couldn’t even

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:50.360
dream of paying these cash salaries.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:56.040
But, thanks to Netflix’s extremely lucrative
subscription model, Netflix can actually afford

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.040
it.

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:59.610
There’s also a bit of strategy to this as
well.

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:03.470
One of the reasons that companies hand out
stock compensation is because they’re hoping

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:06.600
that employees will hang onto it and that
it’ll motivate them.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:11.220
But, the reality is that a substantial number
of employees liquidate their stock as soon

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:12.740
as they receive it.

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:16.669
Even Tim Cook did this despite literally being
CEO.

00:05:16.669 --> 00:05:21.639
Reed approached this from a practical perspective
and figured: why dilute my company and hand

00:05:21.639 --> 00:05:25.960
it out to people that don’t even want it
especially when I can just afford to pay them

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:27.080
in cash?

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:31.729
Really, this line of reasoning makes a lot
of sense and it’s surprising that other

00:05:31.729 --> 00:05:35.750
cash rich companies don’t do the same.

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:40.819
CASH IS KING:
Alright, so that’s why Netflix offers their

00:05:40.819 --> 00:05:46.200
compensations in cash, but is there actually
any real benefit to this?

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:47.900
Well, absolutely.

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:51.009
For one, employees don’t have to deal with
stock fluctuations.

00:05:51.009 --> 00:05:56.259
Here’s the thing, the stock compensation
figures that you see on salary websites aren’t

00:05:56.259 --> 00:05:57.280
constant figures.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:00.199
Let’s take a Facebook director, for example.

00:06:00.199 --> 00:06:05.259
According to levels, a Facebook director earns
$881,000 per year in stock.

00:06:05.259 --> 00:06:10.229
However, this compensation figure only comes
to fruition if the stock price stays constant

00:06:10.229 --> 00:06:11.259
throughout the job.

00:06:11.259 --> 00:06:16.020
In other words, Facebook will not provide
you with more shares of stock just because

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:17.570
the share price falls.

00:06:17.570 --> 00:06:21.180
Instead, a set number of shares are determined
when you get hired.

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:25.590
Usually, these stock plans are setup to last
4 years from the day you get hired.

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:31.440
So, if you get hired as a facebook director,
you wouldn’t be offered $881,000 per year.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:37.130
Rather, you’d be offered $3.5 million for
the next 4 years at current share prices.

00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:40.970
From there, you’ll get access to a portion
of this stock every year.

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:45.190
This might not sound like a big difference,
but it definitely can be.

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:49.849
For example, imagine that you were hired at
Facebook in September of 2021.

00:06:49.849 --> 00:06:56.100
Since then, Facebook stock has plummeted 70%
which would mean that your $881,000 worth

00:06:56.100 --> 00:07:00.060
of stock is only worth $264,000 by the time
you get it.

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:06.139
Now of course, this still adds up to a total
compensation of $700,000, but that’s almost

00:07:06.139 --> 00:07:10.500
half of $1.3 million despite the fact that
you’re doing the same job.

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:14.490
Now, imagine that this same scenario took
place at Netflix.

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:19.710
Netflix stock has also crashed as much as
75% since their peak last year, but if you

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:24.580
were a Netflix director earning $1.3 million
last year, you’d still be earning the full

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:28.060
$1.3 million as long as you didn’t get laid
off of course.

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:31.819
A similar argument could be made for the health
insurance credit as well.

00:07:31.819 --> 00:07:35.930
Usually, a substantial number of employees
don’t even use the company provided health

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:40.080
insurance because they’re covered by their
spouse’s company or their parent’s company

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:42.270
or whatever the case may be.

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:46.789
This is awesome for companies given that they
can boast about having great insurance plans

00:07:46.789 --> 00:07:49.930
while not even having to pay for a bunch of
their employees.

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:54.190
On the flipside, this sucks for employees
given that health insurance is technically

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:58.669
a part of their total compensation, but their
not actually receiving it.

00:07:58.669 --> 00:08:02.409
Netflix’s health insurance credit, however,
would ensure that employees aren’t fully

00:08:02.409 --> 00:08:03.599
left out.

00:08:03.599 --> 00:08:07.669
While employees won’t be able to leverage
the full $15,000 without getting a health

00:08:07.669 --> 00:08:11.590
insurance plan, they can atleast get $5000
in cash.

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:16.669
So, in the end, Netflix’s cash compensation
not only ensures that employees get a stable

00:08:16.669 --> 00:08:21.509
income, but also that they’re able to make
the most of all their perks.

00:08:21.509 --> 00:08:26.970
CASH IS TRASH:
While fully cash compensations have a lot

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:32.250
of perks, like anything, they also come with
a fair number of cons as well.

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:36.700
For example, while cash salaries may seem
nice during recessionary periods, during bull

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:38.800
markets, it’s the exact opposite.

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:41.430
Let’s go back to the Facebook director.

00:08:41.430 --> 00:08:46.620
Instead of assuming that you joined in 2021,
what if we assumed that you joined in 2017.

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:51.959
Between 2017 and 2021, Facebook stock grew
by over 100%.

00:08:51.959 --> 00:08:56.459
This means that if you joined Facebook in
2017, your stock compensation would have grown

00:08:56.459 --> 00:09:02.899
to over $1.7 million in 2021 despite the fact
that you’re doing the same job.

00:09:02.899 --> 00:09:08.200
This would increase your total compensation
from $1.3 million to $2.2 million.

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:13.640
Meanwhile, the Netflix director who joined
in 2017 would still be making $1.3 million.

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:18.209
Now, Netflix is pretty good at increasing
salaries according to market conditions, but

00:09:18.209 --> 00:09:22.530
they definitely wouldn’t be able to match
a rise to $2.2 million because the market

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:25.440
rate didn’t actually increase that much.

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:28.690
Facebook actually “only” paid $1.3 million.

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:33.290
You simply ended up earning $2.2 million because
the stock prices worked in your favor.

00:09:33.290 --> 00:09:39.339
Best case scenario, the market rate may have
increased to $1.4 or $1.5 million and that’s

00:09:39.339 --> 00:09:41.180
what Netflix would be able to match.

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:45.660
So, by taking your entire salary in cash,
you give up the opportunity for exponential

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:47.540
growth to work in your favor.

00:09:47.540 --> 00:09:50.940
Granted, you also eliminate the risk of it
working against you.

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:54.649
But, when we’re talking about a company
like Netflix, we’re talking about giving

00:09:54.649 --> 00:09:56.590
up market leading returns.

00:09:56.590 --> 00:10:00.690
One way to workaround this would be to simply
invest your salary yourself or request for

00:10:00.690 --> 00:10:03.269
a portion of your salary to be given in stock.

00:10:03.269 --> 00:10:06.920
But you wouldn’t be able to buy nearly as
much stock as Facebook or Google would’ve

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:11.250
offered you due to the second shortfall of
cash salaries which is high taxes.

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:15.329
When you’re earning 7 figures in cash, you
can bet that the government is going to tax

00:10:15.329 --> 00:10:16.649
the hell out of you.

00:10:16.649 --> 00:10:20.780
If you’re in California, it’s probably
going to be over 50%.

00:10:20.780 --> 00:10:25.870
Meanwhile, if you had a lot of stock compensation,
you’d still be paying a massive tax bill,

00:10:25.870 --> 00:10:30.120
but maybe you could reduce the percentage
to something closer to 40%.

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:34.709
And when we’re talking about 7 figure salaries,
that’s an extra six figures that you get

00:10:34.709 --> 00:10:35.709
to keep.

00:10:35.709 --> 00:10:40.130
Something else to note is that the unlimited
vacation time is also not as good as it may

00:10:40.130 --> 00:10:41.950
seem at first glance.

00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:46.850
You see, employees generally feel bad when
they take time off in an unlimited PTO environment.

00:10:46.850 --> 00:10:52.160
And on average, they actually end up taking
2 fewer days off per year.

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:57.130
So, Netflix ends up coming off as the nice
employer that offers unlimited time off even

00:10:57.130 --> 00:11:00.820
though that doesn’t actually translate to
more time being taken off.

00:11:00.820 --> 00:11:05.149
Also, most companies have a policy of paying
out accrued PTO when you leave the company

00:11:05.149 --> 00:11:06.329
under good terms.

00:11:06.329 --> 00:11:12.149
For companies with unlimited PTO though, there
is no accrued PTO, so they can avoid paying

00:11:12.149 --> 00:11:13.149
that.

00:11:13.149 --> 00:11:17.670
So, while such policies do have their perks,
they’re by no means perfect either.

00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:23.370
THE NETFLIX WAY:
In the end, Netflix has taken an approach

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:26.330
to compensation that’s extremely uncommon.

00:11:26.330 --> 00:11:30.790
Not only do they pay fat tech salaries but
they pay it all in cash.

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:34.410
This is largely due to their philosophy and
extraordinary profitability.

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:39.510
While there are advantages and disadvantages
to this compensation structure, I very much

00:11:39.510 --> 00:11:42.250
think that’s its a step in the right direction.

00:11:42.250 --> 00:11:45.860
Giving employees more control of how they
get paid is never a bad thing.

00:11:45.860 --> 00:11:50.450
Now of course, without structured health insurance
plans or retirement plans, there’s gonna

00:11:50.450 --> 00:11:54.779
be some bozo who spends all the cash and has
no money left when they have a health emergency

00:11:54.779 --> 00:11:56.490
or are approaching retirement.

00:11:56.490 --> 00:12:01.170
But, for the rest of us who are relatively
sensible and take ownership of our financial

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:06.010
situation, I think this is awesome and I would
be a fan of this system being implemented

00:12:06.010 --> 00:12:09.459
much more frequently, but that’s just what
I think.

00:12:09.459 --> 00:12:12.070
Would you prefer an all cash compensation?

